WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.757
Now we move from text into vector representations.

00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:10.497
For the baseline, we use one Sentence Transformers model for both chunks and questions.

00:00:10.497 --> 00:00:16.252
The important requirement is that both sides are represented in the same embedding space.

00:00:16.252 --> 00:00:19.022
Consider the example on the slide.

00:00:19.022 --> 00:00:24.462
One chunk says that students need an approved internship site and supervisor.

00:00:24.462 --> 00:00:29.378
The question asks what someone should complete before internship registration.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.752
The wording is not identical, but the meaning is similar.

00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:38.682
A good embedding model should place those two pieces of text near each other.

00:00:38.682 --> 00:00:47.417
That semantic behavior is the reason vector retrieval can work even when the user does not repeat the exact wording in

00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:50.487
the source document. For now, keep the model fixed.

00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:53.217
First understand the retrieval pipeline.

00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:59.252
Later, if evaluation is weak, comparing embedding models becomes a meaningful experiment.
